<%/* Unsubscribe — the confirm page and the done page, one template.

     Two states rather than two files because the difference is three
     lines; two files would drift the first time the wording changed.

     ⚠️ THE CONFIRM STEP EXISTS BECAUSE LINKS GET PREFETCHED. Mail
     clients and corporate security gateways follow links to scan them.
     If GET unsubscribed on sight, anybody behind a link scanner would be
     silently removed from the list and neither they nor we would ever
     know. One button is the whole cost of preventing that. */%>
<div class="unsub-wrap">
  <%/* ⚠️ `broken` IS NOT AN ERROR PAGE, IT IS A SECOND ROUTE TO THE SAME
       OUTCOME. Mail clients line-wrap and truncate long URLs routinely,
       so a mangled unsubscribe link is ordinary, not suspicious. The one
       thing this page must never do is leave somebody unable to
       unsubscribe — that is how a polite customer becomes a spam
       complaint. So it says what happened and gives two ways to finish
       the job by hand. */%>
  <% if (typeof broken !== 'undefined' && broken) { %>

    <h1 class="unsub-h1">We couldn't read that link</h1>
    <p class="unsub-lead">
      Email programs sometimes split long links across two lines, which breaks them.
      That is almost certainly what happened here &mdash; nothing is wrong with your account.
    </p>

    <div class="unsub-note">
      <p style="margin:0 0 10px"><strong>We can still take you off the list.</strong></p>
      <p style="margin:0">
        Email <a href="mailto:support@bathroomvanitiesoutlet.com?subject=Unsubscribe%20request">support@bathroomvanitiesoutlet.com</a>
        with the word <em>unsubscribe</em>, or call <a href="tel:+18777771948">(877) 777-1948</a>.
        We will do it the same day.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p class="unsub-lead">
      If you copied the link by hand, try clicking it in the email instead &mdash;
      that usually fixes it.
    </p>

    <a href="/" class="btn btn-navy">Back to the shop</a>

  <% } else if (done) { %>

    <h1 class="unsub-h1">You're unsubscribed</h1>
    <p class="unsub-lead">
      We won't send marketing emails to <strong><%= email %></strong> any more.
      That takes effect immediately.
    </p>

    <%/* Said plainly because it is the thing people actually worry about,
         and because it is the difference between this and Brevo's
         blocklist: order receipts and sign-in codes keep working. */%>
    <div class="unsub-note">
      <p style="margin:0">
        You will still get <strong>emails about orders you place</strong> &mdash; receipts,
        shipping and delivery updates &mdash; and the six-digit code you use to sign in.
        Those are not marketing and this does not switch them off.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p class="unsub-lead">
      Changed your mind? Tick the box next time you check out, or call us on
      <a href="tel:+18777771948">(877) 777-1948</a>.
    </p>

    <a href="/" class="btn btn-navy">Back to the shop</a>

  <% } else { %>

    <h1 class="unsub-h1">Unsubscribe</h1>
    <p class="unsub-lead">
      Stop sending marketing emails to <strong><%= email %></strong>?
    </p>

    <div class="unsub-note">
      <p style="margin:0">
        Order receipts, shipping updates and sign-in codes will keep arriving.
        This only turns off offers and news.
      </p>
    </div>

    <%/* No CSRF token. The HMAC in `t` IS the authorisation: it is
         unguessable, it names the address, and it was delivered to that
         address. Requiring a session token as well would break the
         one-click unsubscribe button in Gmail, which POSTs here with no
         session at all. See the route registration for the exemption. */%>
    <form method="POST" action="/unsubscribe">
      <input type="hidden" name="t" value="<%= t %>">
      <button type="submit" class="btn btn-navy">Yes, unsubscribe me</button>
    </form>

    <p class="unsub-small"><a href="/">No thanks, take me back</a></p>

  <% } %>
</div>
